Linux's drm/imagination in the powervr driver
=============================================

The files here are copies of Linux's drm/imagination (VERSION says which
commit), used under the MIT licence of their "GPL-2.0-only OR MIT" dual
licence. They are compiled as GNU C, unchanged but for the one change below,
against compat/linux_compat.h: every <linux/...>, <drm/...> and <asm/...>
header they include is a one-line stub in compat/ that includes it.

Where Linux's DRM core and the files built on it would be, glue/ has
native code with the same interfaces:

  glue/pvr_gem.h          buffer objects on locked, write-combined areas
                          (replaces pvr_gem.h; GEM shmem does not exist)
  glue/pvr_queue.h,       job queues and SUBMIT_JOBS without drm_sched
  pvr_job.h, pvr_trace.h  (replace pvr_queue.h, pvr_job.h, pvr_trace.h)
  glue/pvr_haiku_drv.c    compiles pvr_drv.c unchanged and hands its
                          struct drm_driver to the dispatcher
  glue/pvr_haiku_drm.c    the DRM file and ioctl layer (drm_file.c,
                          drm_ioctl.c): opens, the generic ioctls Mesa uses,
                          MAP_BO in place of mmap()
  glue/pvr_haiku_sync.c   DRM sync objects, binary and timeline
                          (drm_syncobj.c), on compat's dma_fence
  glue/pvr_haiku_vm.c     GPU VM contexts on pvr_mmu.c's page tables
                          (replaces pvr_vm.c, which is built on drm_gpuvm)
  glue/pvr_haiku_*.c      the device (pvr_device.c, pvr_drv.c's probe),
                          buffers (pvr_gem.c), job queues (pvr_queue.c,
                          pvr_job.c), power-off and device-lost
                          (pvr_power.c), and stubs for what this GPU never
                          reaches (META and RISC-V firmware processors)
  glue/pvr_haiku_glue.h   the C interface PvrDevice.cpp and driver.cpp use

Build flags for the C files (Jamfile): -Wno-pointer-arith -Wno-sign-compare
-Wno-pointer-sign, which Linux builds with and the upstream code relies on.
The structure layout checks (pvr_rogue_*_check.h) are compiled in, so a
layout that differs from Linux's fails the build.


Local changes
-------------

pvr_fw.c, pvr_fw_init(): when the firmware does not report itself started
within 5 s, pvr_haiku_fw_boot_failed() is called before pvr_fw_stop() and
the clean-up: it logs the registers, the firmware's shared structures, the
MIPS boot stage and the firmware trace while that memory still exists, and
puts the GPU into soft reset. Three lines, marked "Haiku":

	+/* Haiku: logs the device state while the firmware's memory still exists. */
	+void pvr_haiku_fw_boot_failed(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev);
	...
	 		drm_err(from_pvr_device(pvr_dev), "Firmware failed to boot\n");
	+		pvr_haiku_fw_boot_failed(pvr_dev); /* Haiku */
	 		goto err_fw_stop;


Updating
--------

Copy the same files from a newer Linux commit, update VERSION, re-apply
the change above, build. New Linux APIs show up as compile errors and go
into compat/linux_compat.h; new calls into the glue as link errors (the
kernel refuses an add-on with undefined symbols, so check with nm -D).
